#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f7f36b-4e9c-5a86-bd20-ca01d9fce41f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9412" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2$. What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x< 3$ | $x> 3$ |" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1$. These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/b7c9a28da4eb9d34bd96f0d44ec1e023.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/59e7d4a360af087d0af49993c2fcb8b8.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/c0d3dfb30ea814e83cccbb4b403b88fa.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x< 3$ | $x> 3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "222d8f84-be89-5bcf-94f3-f309635bbcbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9414" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1$. These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/1d27c3483fe5a41e0d1d8b6a1156162a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/e1a79695a81263b0d3e9b0ff97ee32c2.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/a492e76f5893f613b3bf5fdabaebbfa9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0642538-6b85-5e67-9f42-396e0fb3dd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177251} {:answer/id #uuid "df09ae57-bd34-542c-a436-1c558fbc4407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177252} {:answer/id #uuid "10ee1709-b0de-5ae3-886f-fd6c092f567e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177253} {:answer/id #uuid "43cd8039-c23b-5ddb-879a-f4abc6b5b3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177254} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9856c0-486f-5b7f-8994-065089dccd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ee1709-b0de-5ae3-886f-fd6c092f567e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177253} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb8638e-fe07-56fe-bff6-9e8706b5887f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42cb2492-ecd7-59e3-9c43-d91c9543c6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-121090" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) \\ge 0$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The product $x(y+1)$ is nonnegative when its factors have the same sign or at least one is zero. Thus the solution is\n$$\\{x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge-1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{x\\le0,\\ y\\le-1\\}.$$\nChoose the diagram shading the upper-right and lower-left regions relative to $x=0$ and $y=-1$, with both boundary lines included. These boundaries contain all points where the product is zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db8b98c3-4856-53e6-9697-149247cc060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177257} {:answer/id #uuid "e260a6fb-fdfc-5be5-b6b6-5c714c5e151b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177258} {:answer/id #uuid "022ee7a9-4f27-55e0-9831-4674bce7c31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177259} {:answer/id #uuid "1b944579-e38c-532a-bdc7-45a937ed1f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177260} {:answer/id #uuid "51478ed4-48e2-5e1f-afe2-45a2128b3dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "022ee7a9-4f27-55e0-9831-4674bce7c31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6037a26f-e055-55ef-8673-5d538b998a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c2dc9d-e99c-5a65-8e17-97fc8f7373ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-121092" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x^{2}(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) < 0$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "When $x\\ne0$, the factor $x^2$ is positive, so $x^2(y+1)<0$ precisely when $y+1<0$. At $x=0$, the product is zero and fails the strict inequality. The solution is\n$$y<-1,\\qquad x\\ne0.$$\nChoose the diagram shading below $y=-1$ on both sides of the excluded line $x=0$, with both boundaries dashed."}]}
